Iterate CottList directly; handle final union variants without a redundant match.
cott-domain real.posting.client.send_request return: Verified facts: http.client treats EOF inside the header block as the end of headers, and HTTPResponse.read(amt) returns short data at EOF without IncompleteRead, so http.client used as-is cannot meet the truncation rules in the doc (closed before the empty line, before all Content-Length bytes, or before the terminating chunk must be NetworkFailed); urllib's default opener follows POST redirects and cannot be restricted without defining a class, and generated Python implementations may not define classes. urllib.parse.urljoin is not strict RFC 3986 (urljoin("http://a/p/q", "..//g") gives http://a/g but RFC 3986 section 5.2 gives http://a//g; network-path references keep their dot segments), so implement section 5.2 resolution and 5.2.4 remove_dot_segments by hand. bytes.decode("utf-8", "replace") already replaces each maximal subpart of an ill-formed sequence with one U+FFFD (Unicode Table 3-8) and keeps a BOM and NUL. A hand-written HTTP/1.1 client over socket and ssl (create_connection with timeout_ms, ssl default context for https) is one workable design; the design is otherwise open. The scenario HTTP fixture is a real loopback server reached by this same client; cott_runtime._cott_fixture_http returns only a GET body and is not used here.
